WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.334 --> 00:00:02.735
-Our first guest tonight
is an Emmy-winning writer

00:00:02.735 --> 00:00:05.204
and an Emmy- and
Golden Globe-nominated actor.

00:00:05.204 --> 00:00:08.207
His children's book, "Zilot
&amp; Other Important Rhymes,"

00:00:08.207 --> 00:00:09.042
is on sale now.

00:00:09.042 --> 00:00:10.009
Please welcome back to the show,

00:00:10.009 --> 00:00:11.711
our friend Bob Odenkirk,
everybody.

00:00:11.711 --> 00:00:14.714
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:14.714 --> 00:00:27.694 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:27.694 --> 00:00:28.962
Welcome back, Bob.

00:00:28.962 --> 00:00:30.597
-Thank you, man.
Great to be here.

00:00:30.597 --> 00:00:32.966
-You are a SAG member.
You're a WGA member.

00:00:32.966 --> 00:00:35.168
You were very active
on the picket lines this summer.

00:00:35.168 --> 00:00:37.036
-I really was.
That's your job, right?

00:00:37.036 --> 00:00:38.771
-That is your job.
And you did a great job.

00:00:38.771 --> 00:00:40.373
-Well, I --
This is my third strike.

00:00:40.373 --> 00:00:42.475
-Congratulations.
-Thank you.

00:00:42.475 --> 00:00:45.078
[ Chuckles ]
Badge of honor.

00:00:45.078 --> 00:00:47.914
I -- You know,
I know what to do.

00:00:47.914 --> 00:00:50.850
You go out to where
the other writers are

00:00:50.850 --> 00:00:53.653
and you walk in circles,
and that's what you do.

00:00:53.653 --> 00:00:55.054
And that's your part
of the job.

00:00:55.054 --> 00:00:57.590
-Did you find that
as you get older,

00:00:57.590 --> 00:01:00.860
you realize that if you walk in
the same direction for too long,

00:01:00.860 --> 00:01:02.962
one hip just starts to hurt?
-Yes. [ Laughs ]

00:01:02.962 --> 00:01:06.165
-Yeah. I really -- I'm like,
"Guys, we got to switch it up.

00:01:06.165 --> 00:01:08.100
This is -- We're going to be
feeling this tomorrow."

00:01:08.100 --> 00:01:11.404
-That's right. And if you
turn around and go the other way

00:01:11.404 --> 00:01:14.474
or you just switch people
that you're with,

00:01:14.474 --> 00:01:17.910
then you get other
screenplays...from them.

00:01:17.910 --> 00:01:19.312
-Do you like the camaraderie
of the picket line?

00:01:19.312 --> 00:01:20.746
-I love the camaraderie.
-It's really nice.

00:01:20.746 --> 00:01:25.118
I mean, writers kind of
are loners, sort of.

00:01:25.118 --> 00:01:26.886
They kind of are in their lives.

00:01:26.886 --> 00:01:28.187
But then you put them in a line

00:01:28.187 --> 00:01:31.124
and give them something to hate
together,

00:01:31.124 --> 00:01:35.094
they become best buds,
and we're all chums.

00:01:35.094 --> 00:01:37.196
-You're a pro now,
but did you feel like a rookie

00:01:37.196 --> 00:01:40.699
in the '88 strike?
-Oh, I was such an ass[bleep].

00:01:40.699 --> 00:01:43.570
-Uh-huh.
-I was such a jerk.

00:01:43.570 --> 00:01:45.804
Like, I had been hired
at "Saturday Night Live"

00:01:45.804 --> 00:01:49.809
only a few shows
before the strike.

00:01:49.809 --> 00:01:53.479
What right did I have?
[ Chuckles ]

00:01:53.479 --> 00:01:57.183
And -- And I go to the strike
with my friend Robert Smigel.

00:01:57.183 --> 00:01:58.884
-Fantastic writer.
-One of the great

00:01:58.884 --> 00:02:00.486
writers of all time.

00:02:00.486 --> 00:02:04.089
And we get there
and somebody is speaking.

00:02:04.089 --> 00:02:07.861
Somebody is giving a speech.
"We can do this! Hang in there!"

00:02:07.861 --> 00:02:10.429
Some old guy is going on and on.

00:02:10.429 --> 00:02:11.997
And I'm like,
"Who's this ass[bleep]?

00:02:11.997 --> 00:02:14.066
This guy's a jerk.
Shut up already.

00:02:14.066 --> 00:02:15.401
Let's start walking."

00:02:15.401 --> 00:02:17.469
Somebody leans over
to me and goes,

00:02:17.469 --> 00:02:19.772
"That's Budd Schulberg" --

00:02:19.772 --> 00:02:23.542
one of the great writers
of the century.

00:02:23.542 --> 00:02:27.513
And here I am,
this jerk from Naperville,

00:02:27.513 --> 00:02:28.748
you know, giving him [bleep].

00:02:28.748 --> 00:02:32.885
But I felt duly chastised,
and --

00:02:32.885 --> 00:02:35.088
and I carried on with the job.

00:02:35.088 --> 00:02:38.424
-You talked about
your early "SNL" time,

00:02:38.424 --> 00:02:41.227
and you wrote a great book
about your career in comedy.

00:02:41.227 --> 00:02:43.029
-Oh, thank you.
-And I heard you recently

00:02:43.029 --> 00:02:44.864
tell the story,
and this actually has

00:02:44.864 --> 00:02:47.667
some lore
about talking to a host,

00:02:47.667 --> 00:02:49.034
because sometimes
you have to talk

00:02:49.034 --> 00:02:51.337
to really accomplished actors.
-This was a great actor.

00:02:51.337 --> 00:02:53.740
Jeremy Irons.
-I mean, to this day.

00:02:53.740 --> 00:02:56.109
-One of the greats.
-And what was your --

00:02:56.109 --> 00:02:57.277
what was your --
-My experience

00:02:57.277 --> 00:03:01.213
was that I had written
a monologue for him

00:03:01.213 --> 00:03:03.282
with someone else,
another writer.

00:03:03.282 --> 00:03:05.985
And it was -- It took us,
you know,

00:03:05.985 --> 00:03:08.854
a good 26 minutes to write it.

00:03:08.854 --> 00:03:12.091
A lot of work went into it,
and it's his monologue

00:03:12.091 --> 00:03:13.593
and he's been waiting
to do the show.

00:03:13.593 --> 00:03:17.496
And he was so mad.
And he's reading it like this.

00:03:17.496 --> 00:03:19.198
And I'm sitting right here.
He's right here.

00:03:19.198 --> 00:03:20.899
His face is right here,

00:03:20.899 --> 00:03:23.403
and he's looking at this thing
and he's going,

00:03:23.403 --> 00:03:27.239
"You're making me do this?
I mean..."

00:03:27.239 --> 00:03:29.676
And he starts complaining

00:03:29.676 --> 00:03:32.778
and I have my recorder
and I press record,

00:03:32.778 --> 00:03:34.480
and I get this monologue.

00:03:34.480 --> 00:03:36.782
Which camera have I got?

00:03:36.782 --> 00:03:42.888
"I mean, I can dance,
I can sing,

00:03:42.888 --> 00:03:45.224
I can do Shakespeare,

00:03:45.224 --> 00:03:46.759
I can juggle,

00:03:46.759 --> 00:03:48.427
I can do anything,

00:03:48.427 --> 00:03:50.897
and you have me do this?!"
[ Laughter ]

00:03:50.897 --> 00:03:55.401
And, oh, it was the greatest.
It was so great. I loved it.

00:03:55.401 --> 00:03:57.403
And then he had
to do that monologue.

00:03:57.403 --> 00:04:00.072
[ Laughter ]

00:04:00.072 --> 00:04:02.074
He did.

00:04:02.074 --> 00:04:04.610
-I like -- Does that mean
you went back to Lorne's office,

00:04:04.610 --> 00:04:05.744
and he's like,
"Does he like it?"

00:04:05.744 --> 00:04:06.712
And you're like,
"He's good to go."

00:04:06.712 --> 00:04:09.282
-"He loves it! He's on board."

00:04:09.282 --> 00:04:11.250
-So, this is fantastic.

00:04:11.250 --> 00:04:12.685
This is a book that has been,

00:04:12.685 --> 00:04:14.453
I mean, two decades
in the making.

00:04:14.453 --> 00:04:15.888
-It is. It is.

00:04:15.888 --> 00:04:18.991
Zilot is a word
invented by my son Nate

00:04:18.991 --> 00:04:22.561
when he was about six years old
for a blanket fort.

00:04:22.561 --> 00:04:24.998
Okay?
You build blanket forts, right?

00:04:24.998 --> 00:04:26.866
-Yeah, of course.
-With kids, they love it.

00:04:26.866 --> 00:04:29.535
And you put a blanket
over a chair and a couch,

00:04:29.535 --> 00:04:30.770
get inside, read books,

00:04:30.770 --> 00:04:33.106
and the cat comes in
and it's the best.

00:04:33.106 --> 00:04:34.607
And...

00:04:34.607 --> 00:04:37.409
And my kids, we would read
a lot of books before bedtime.

00:04:37.409 --> 00:04:38.611
And at a certain point, I said,

00:04:38.611 --> 00:04:41.180
"Let's start
writing some poems."

00:04:41.180 --> 00:04:44.550
And so we wrote a bunch
of really silly poems

00:04:44.550 --> 00:04:46.152
and I kept them on a shelf.

00:04:46.152 --> 00:04:49.087
And then 15 years later,
here comes a pandemic.

00:04:49.087 --> 00:04:50.923
My daughter is an artist.

00:04:50.923 --> 00:04:53.392
-So she drew all the pictures.
-She did all the illustrations,

00:04:53.392 --> 00:04:55.561
and they're really beautiful.
-She did a wonderful job.

00:04:55.561 --> 00:04:59.966
-Yeah. And we just sat down
and rewrote the poems

00:04:59.966 --> 00:05:02.135
so they were good, because...
[ Laughter ]

00:05:02.135 --> 00:05:04.637
-So you do have the original --
-I have the original book.

00:05:04.637 --> 00:05:07.072
-This is what you kept on a
shelf, which is very impressive.

00:05:07.072 --> 00:05:08.341
-This is what
I kept on the shelf.

00:05:08.341 --> 00:05:10.409
-Yeah.
-"Old Time Rhymes" I called it.

00:05:10.409 --> 00:05:13.578
I wanted the kids to feel like
they had really written a book.

00:05:13.578 --> 00:05:15.314
By Dad, Nate and Erin.

00:05:15.314 --> 00:05:18.151
And the truth is, look,
there were --

00:05:18.151 --> 00:05:21.220
I knew there were, like,
10 good ones in here,

00:05:21.220 --> 00:05:23.622
but most of the time
they're like this.

00:05:23.622 --> 00:05:25.391
And here's one I just
picked out for you just

00:05:25.391 --> 00:05:28.927
to show you my kids were not
born magically delicious.

00:05:28.927 --> 00:05:31.597
[ Laughter ]
This one's called "Stay up."

00:05:31.597 --> 00:05:34.066
[ Clears throat ]
"Eat some jello.

00:05:34.066 --> 00:05:35.234
Go play the cello."

00:05:35.234 --> 00:05:37.636
See those...?
[ Laughter ]

00:05:37.636 --> 00:05:41.240
"Go out on the street
and meet an odd fellow.

00:05:41.240 --> 00:05:44.510
Put a splash of toothpaste
in your comb if you dare,

00:05:44.510 --> 00:05:48.447
so you can clean its teeth
while you fix your hair."

00:05:48.447 --> 00:05:50.183
-Not terrible.
-Not terrible.

00:05:50.183 --> 00:05:52.885
-Not terrible.
-Kind of...

00:05:52.885 --> 00:05:56.055
Kind of clunky.
-So you obviously come back.

00:05:56.055 --> 00:05:57.656
So it's pandemic.
You got the kids in the house.

00:05:57.656 --> 00:06:00.593
-So I'm rewriting
one or two or three a week

00:06:00.593 --> 00:06:02.161
and I'm knocking on
my daughter's door

00:06:02.161 --> 00:06:05.498
like it's a, you know,
"30 Rock," an office.

00:06:05.498 --> 00:06:08.334
"Where are those drawings?
Let's go.

00:06:08.334 --> 00:06:10.603
You said you'd
have them yesterday!

00:06:10.603 --> 00:06:11.804
Ahh."
-And what about Nate?

00:06:11.804 --> 00:06:13.672
Was Nate sort of
getting off scot free?

00:06:13.672 --> 00:06:16.442
-No, he. -- My son. I was like,
"I need 10 more poems."

00:06:16.442 --> 00:06:18.611
-[ Laughs ]
When they were that age,

00:06:18.611 --> 00:06:20.646
when they were writing poems
with you back then,

00:06:20.646 --> 00:06:21.914
were they aware
that you were funny?

00:06:21.914 --> 00:06:22.815
Were they entertained by you?

00:06:22.815 --> 00:06:25.184
-Um...yeah.
-Okay.

00:06:25.184 --> 00:06:27.719
-I was trying to be funny.

00:06:27.719 --> 00:06:29.655
I was very silly
around the kids.

00:06:29.655 --> 00:06:34.093
Really fun to do that.
And they kind of wised up.

00:06:34.093 --> 00:06:36.695
They loved my silliness.
-Right.

00:06:36.695 --> 00:06:40.132
-But one time when my daughter
was six, it was so great.

00:06:40.132 --> 00:06:42.634
I was doing a character
in the mirror.

00:06:42.634 --> 00:06:44.503
"Yeah, yeah, yeah,
time to go to school!

00:06:44.503 --> 00:06:47.506
Let's go! Get on the bus!
Put on your clothes!"

00:06:47.506 --> 00:06:50.243
And -- And I stopped,
and she goes...

00:06:50.243 --> 00:06:51.344
"Bad."

00:06:51.344 --> 00:06:54.280
[ Laughter ]

00:06:54.280 --> 00:06:57.616
Six year old. You know?
I loved it.

00:06:57.616 --> 00:06:59.185
-And you started
your recorder again.

00:06:59.185 --> 00:07:00.620
You're like...
-"Come on."

00:07:00.620 --> 00:07:03.222
-"I'd like to get this on tape."
-"I got a monologue for you."

00:07:03.222 --> 00:07:06.925
-I do know that there are
a lot of kids books these days.

00:07:06.925 --> 00:07:09.461
I would imagine it's a very
tough market to sell one.

00:07:09.461 --> 00:07:10.463
-Well, usually it is.

00:07:10.463 --> 00:07:12.831
But this month
we got very lucky.

00:07:12.831 --> 00:07:16.169
All the other kids books
coming out are just terrible.

00:07:16.169 --> 00:07:17.870
[ Laughter ]
-Now...

00:07:17.870 --> 00:07:19.772
we didn't believe you
when we heard...

00:07:19.772 --> 00:07:22.708
-Yeah, they're awful.
-And so we went and --

00:07:22.708 --> 00:07:24.477
And you had mentioned --
-And you bought a few.

00:07:24.477 --> 00:07:27.012
You just randomly bought --
-I couldn't believe

00:07:27.012 --> 00:07:28.514
that these were actual titles,

00:07:28.514 --> 00:07:30.182
because you did hit
the jackpot that your book --

00:07:30.182 --> 00:07:32.218
-I just got lucky.
I just got lucky.

00:07:32.218 --> 00:07:34.053
-Like, here's -- here's
a new book that is out.

00:07:34.053 --> 00:07:35.421
-This one's coming out.
-It's called

00:07:35.421 --> 00:07:37.990
"Nothing But Sharp Edges."
[ Laughter ]

00:07:37.990 --> 00:07:39.691
-Why would you make this?
-I mean, show --

00:07:39.691 --> 00:07:40.960
Do you want
to show them what...?

00:07:40.960 --> 00:07:43.261
-Why would you make
this book for children?

00:07:43.261 --> 00:07:45.898
Why? Why would you...?

00:07:45.898 --> 00:07:48.334
Why?
-That's crazy!

00:07:48.334 --> 00:07:49.534
-Why would you...?

00:07:49.534 --> 00:07:52.137
This is not -- This is...

00:07:52.137 --> 00:07:53.439
This is not...
-I mean, yeah,

00:07:53.439 --> 00:07:56.742
if it's like, look,
even if I don't like poems,

00:07:56.742 --> 00:07:57.943
like, it's a no-brainer.

00:07:57.943 --> 00:08:00.679
-I'd go with the poetry book.
-And then this...

00:08:00.679 --> 00:08:04.516
And, again, I understand there's
a lot of books about, like,

00:08:04.516 --> 00:08:06.718
going to the bathroom
is a hard thing for kids to get.

00:08:06.718 --> 00:08:09.822
So I'm not judging that it's
about going to the bathroom.

00:08:09.822 --> 00:08:13.793
But "That's right, Esme,
the Toilet Has Teeth."

00:08:13.793 --> 00:08:16.428
-Why would you teach...?
Why would you show kids...?

00:08:16.428 --> 00:08:19.164
That's not a thing
that you want to do.

00:08:19.164 --> 00:08:20.599
-Yeah!
I would think this would --

00:08:20.599 --> 00:08:22.568
-This is our competition.
-Yeah.

00:08:22.568 --> 00:08:24.303
-We're golden.
-What a good month.

00:08:24.303 --> 00:08:26.738
-Yes.
-And then sometimes, like,

00:08:26.738 --> 00:08:31.176
there's, like, promotional
tie-ins with film or TV.

00:08:31.176 --> 00:08:32.645
-Sure.
-And I think that pays off.

00:08:32.645 --> 00:08:34.847
You know, if there's a big kids
movie coming out and, like,

00:08:34.847 --> 00:08:37.049
you get a book and, you know,
the kids get to see it.

00:08:37.049 --> 00:08:40.119
But I can't imagine that
there's any parent out there

00:08:40.119 --> 00:08:45.457
that wants to get their kid
"Li'l Oppenheimer

00:08:45.457 --> 00:08:47.493
and His Big Booming Bomb."

00:08:47.493 --> 00:08:49.094
-Why would you want
to give the kid that?

00:08:49.094 --> 00:08:52.064
That's not a good thing
for the kid to have!

00:08:52.064 --> 00:08:53.398
-It's not a good thing for kids!

00:08:53.398 --> 00:08:55.167
[ Both speaking shrilly ]

00:08:55.167 --> 00:08:56.435
-Go to the last page of this.

00:08:56.435 --> 00:08:57.970
-I don't even want to,
because I am like,

00:08:57.970 --> 00:08:59.872
"How do you even teach kids...?"
-This is for children.

00:08:59.872 --> 00:09:01.239
-No!
[ Laughter ]

00:09:01.239 --> 00:09:02.541
You're telling me this is

00:09:02.541 --> 00:09:04.409
the last page of this book?
-Show them.

00:09:04.409 --> 00:09:05.845
-I mean, I will,
but I'm worried.

00:09:05.845 --> 00:09:08.246
-"...and everybody will die.

00:09:08.246 --> 00:09:10.015
Everybody.

00:09:10.015 --> 00:09:14.053
The end."
-I mean, what? Come on.

00:09:14.053 --> 00:09:16.088
I mean, it's "Zilot"
or nothing this month.

00:09:16.088 --> 00:09:17.890
This month,
it's "Zilot" or nothing.

00:09:17.890 --> 00:09:19.558
You guys, Bob Odenkirk!

00:09:19.558 --> 00:09:21.794
[ Cheers and applause ]
"Zilot" is on sale now.

00:09:21.794 --> 00:09:24.530
We'll be right back with "SNL's"
Marcello Hernandez.

